    "abstract": "<jats:title>Abstract</jats:title>\n                  <jats:p>Black liquor is a typical wastewater and troublesome pollutant in the papermaking industry, which is rich in lignin, hemicellulose, and residual alkali. Here, we developed a short and efficient process to prepare spherical hierarchical porous activated carbon from papermaking black liquor. The precursor was prepared by spray pyrolysis of black liquor, where carbonization of black liquor, preliminary activation, and pore formation are achieved under the synergistic effects of high temperature, water vapor, and sodium/potassium hydroxides. The further activation was carried out using the residual sodium and potassium compounds in the precursor material as etching agents. Hierarchical porous amorphous carbon was achieved with the merits of ultrathin-walled (10–20 nm) porous hollow spheres and a large amount of thin, bending, and reticular carbon nanosheets. Owing to its unique morphology, microstructure, and hierarchical pores, the optimized sample AC700 presents a large specific surface area of 2104.3 m2 g–1 and a pore volume of 1.139 cm3 g–1. When evaluated as the cathode material of lithium-ion capacitors, AC700 presents a high specific capacity of 67.3 mAh g–1 within 2.0–4.0 V, along with excellent rate capability and cycling stability. This work offers an efficient and scalable pathway for high-value utilization of papermaking black liquor.</jats:p>",
